  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Georgia totaled $6.5B in FY2026 to date. Those obligations came in 58,282 awards, with an average award size of $112K, and obligations fell 44.1% year over year. Annual obligations have varied: FY21 was $11.1B, FY22 was $8.5B, FY23 was $11B, FY24 was $10.4B, and FY25 was $11.6B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3.6B so far, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services at $1B and the General Services Administration at $771M. Top vendors include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP with $558M and MANHATTAN TORCON A JOINT VENTURE with $492M. Major industry categories in Georgia are Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $927M, Aircraft Manufacturing at $629M, and Engineering Services at $571M. This mix shows heavy defense and construction spending alongside professional services and R&D, and the current-year totals are partial because they reflect fiscal year to date obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Georgia?",
        "body": "Buying is concentrated. The Department of Defense accounts for $3.6B of obligations, and the next two buyers are the Department of Health and Human Services at $1B and the General Services Administration at $771M. The top industries combine large single-category totals, including Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $927M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $629M. That concentration means a few agencies and industry sectors account for a large share of obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What does this mean for contractors competing in Georgia?",
        "body": "Competition is high across many award sizes: there were 58,282 awards with an average award size of $112K. Prime opportunities are visible in defense, construction, engineering, and computer systems design, which show multi-hundred-million dollar totals. Contractors should note obligations fell 44.1% year over year and FY26 totals are partial, so activity levels this fiscal year may not match prior full-year totals."
      }
    ]
  },
